[[:db/retract 17592186343034 :question/answer-fields 17592186343039] {:db/id "q-342644/mathematical-correction-v1/field-2/answer-0" :answer/id #uuid "3714ef30-6f75-5fa4-988a-c62c4c89d1b3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342644/mathematical-correction-v1/field-2/answer-1" :answer/id #uuid "15575576-7219-5852-bb31-1ae68bfbb0c9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342644/mathematical-correction-v1/field-2/answer-2" :answer/id #uuid "510ee0da-7a3b-5ea9-b9df-6d0fbee571af" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342644/mathematical-correction-v1/field-2/answer-3" :answer/id #uuid "8d757184-c59c-59ff-9d73-ebdd4e1e4370" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "outside the real coefficient domain" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342644/mathematical-correction-v1/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "c586ee04-a0c2-506c-8bc2-428a175dab8c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/choices ["q-342644/mathematical-correction-v1/field-2/answer-0" "q-342644/mathematical-correction-v1/field-2/answer-1" "q-342644/mathematical-correction-v1/field-2/answer-2" "q-342644/mathematical-correction-v1/field-2/answer-3"] :answer-field/correct "q-342644/mathematical-correction-v1/field-2/answer-3" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186343034 :question/problem "$$\nx(x-9)(x+1){y}^{″}+(x-9){y}^{′}+x(x+1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y=0\n$$\n\nClassify the following points for the differential equation given above.$\\,$The point $x=3$ is\n{{field-1}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=-1$ is\n{{field-2}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=0$ is\n{{field-3}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=9$ is\n{{field-4}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\nWork over the reals. Use the classification \"outside the real coefficient domain\" at points separated from the coefficient domain. At a boundary of the coefficient domain, classify singularity by whether the normalized coefficients, or their required weighted forms, admit analytic extension from that domain." :question/worked-solution "For the original real logarithm $\\ln x$, the coefficient domain is $x>0$. Normalize the equation on that domain away from its zero leading coefficient:\n\n$$y''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0,\\qquad P(x)=\\frac1{x(x+1)},\\quad Q(x)=\\frac{\\ln x}{x-9}.$$\n\nAt $x=3$, both normalized coefficients are analytic, so the point is ordinary.\n\nThe point $x=-1$ lies outside the real coefficient domain. There is no real neighborhood of $-1$ on which $\\ln x$ is defined, and it is not a boundary point approached from $x>0$. Therefore the correct classification is \"outside the real coefficient domain\". Calling it a real regular or irregular singular point would presuppose a local differential equation that is not defined there.\n\nThe point $x=0$ is a singular boundary point. From $x>0$, $xP=1/(x+1)$ has an analytic extension, but $x^2Q=x^2\\ln x/(x-9)$ does not: its second derivative has leading divergent term $-(2/9)\\ln x$. Therefore zero is irregular singular under the stated analytic extension criterion. A finite weighted-coefficient limit alone does not establish analyticity.\n\nAt $x=9$, $Q$ has a pole, while $(x-9)P$ and $(x-9)^2Q=(x-9)\\ln x$ are analytic. Therefore $x=9$ is regular singular.\n\nReplacing the original coefficient by $\\ln|x|$ would define a different equation and make $-1$ regular singular." :db/ensure [:question/validate] :question/answer-fields ["q-342644/mathematical-correction-v1/field-2"]}]
